select public.fd_ai_upsert_chunks('fd-textbook', '1b1a21163698', 16, $aim_json$[{"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:16.0#1", "source_key": "fd-textbook:16.0", "chapter": 16, "section": "16.0", "heading": "Overview", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 1, "title": "Chapter 16, Numerical Methods: Trees, Simulation, and Finite Differences — 16.0 Overview", "body": "Breeden, D. T., and Litzenberger, R. H. (1978). Prices of state-contingent claims implicit in option prices. Journal of Business, 51(4), 621–651.\nDerman, E., and Kani, I. (1994). Riding on a smile. Risk, 7(2), 32–39.\nDupire, B. (1994). Pricing with a smile. Risk, 7(1), 18–20.\nGatheral, J. (2006). The Volatility Surface: A Practitioner's Guide. Wiley.\nHull, J. C. (2022). Options, Futures, and Other Derivatives (11th ed.). Pearson. Chapter 20.\nRubinstein, M. (1994). Implied binomial trees. Journal of Finance, 49(3), 771–818."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:16.0#2", "source_key": "fd-textbook:16.0", "chapter": 16, "section": "16.0", "heading": "Decide first", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 2, "title": "Chapter 16, Numerical Methods: Trees, Simulation, and Finite Differences — 16.0 Decide first", "body": "⚙ Decision — choose first; the theory follows\nHow many more trials?\nAn analyst values an option by simulation. With 10,000 simulated outcomes the estimate of the value has a standard error of $0.04. The trading desk requires a standard error of $0.01. How many outcomes must be simulated?"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:16.1#3", "source_key": "fd-textbook:16.1", "chapter": 16, "section": "16.1", "heading": "The need for numerical methods", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 3, "title": "Chapter 16, Numerical Methods: Trees, Simulation, and Finite Differences — 16.1 The need for numerical methods", "body": "The Black–Scholes–Merton formulas value European calls and puts. Most other derivatives have no formula. The American put of Chapter 10 has none, because the holder may exercise at any time and the value depends on when it is optimal to do so. An option whose payoff depends on the average price over a period has none. An option on several assets has none. Such derivatives are valued by numerical methods, which are procedures that produce an approximate value and can be made as accurate as required by additional computation.\nThree methods are in general use. All three apply the principle of risk-neutral valuation, Principle 10.1, and differ in how they represent the uncertain future.\nA tree represents the possible prices of the asset at a sequence of dates and works backward from expiration to the present.\nMonte Carlo simulation draws a large number of possible paths of the price at random, computes the payoff on each, and averages. It works forward from the present to expiration.\nA finite difference method solves the Black–Scholes–Merton differential equation, Result 12.1, on a grid of prices and dates. Like a tree, it works backward."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:16.1#4", "source_key": "fd-textbook:16.1", "chapter": 16, "section": "16.1", "heading": "The need for numerical methods", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 4, "title": "Chapter 16, Numerical Methods: Trees, Simulation, and Finite Differences — 16.1 The need for numerical methods", "body": "The direction in which a method works determines what it can value. At each date the holder of an American option compares the value of exercising with the value of continuing. The value of continuing is known only when later dates have been dealt with, so that a method that works backward handles early exercise naturally. A payoff that depends on the path of the price, such as an average, is known only when the path has been followed from the start, so that a method that works forward handles it naturally. Table 16.1 summarizes.\nTable 16.1 The three numerical methods compared\nBinomial treeMonte Carlo simulationFinite differences\nDirectionBackwardForwardBackward\nEarly exerciseHandled directlyRequires special techniquesHandled directly\nPayoff depending on the pathDifficultHandled directlyDifficult\nSeveral underlying variablesImpractical beyond two or threeCost rises only in proportion to their numberImpractical beyond two or three\nErrorFalls roughly in proportion to the number of stepsFalls with the square root of the number of trials; it is random and can be measuredFalls with the fineness of the grid"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:16.1#5", "source_key": "fd-textbook:16.1", "chapter": 16, "section": "16.1", "heading": "The need for numerical methods", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 5, "title": "Chapter 16, Numerical Methods: Trees, Simulation, and Finite Differences — 16.1 The need for numerical methods", "body": "SensitivitiesDelta, gamma, and theta from the tree itselfRequire repeated simulationDelta, gamma, and theta from the grid itself"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:16.2#6", "source_key": "fd-textbook:16.2", "chapter": 16, "section": "16.2", "heading": "Binomial trees in practice", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 6, "title": "Chapter 16, Numerical Methods: Trees, Simulation, and Finite Differences — 16.2 Binomial trees in practice", "body": "Chapter 10 developed the binomial tree. With a time step of \\(\\Delta t\\), the tree of Cox, Ross, and Rubinstein, Result 10.2, sets \\[ u=e^{\\sigma\\sqrt{\\Delta t}}, \\qquad d=\\frac1u, \\qquad p=\\frac{a-d}{u-d}, \\qquad a=e^{(r-q)\\Delta t}, \\] where \\(q\\) is the rate of income on the asset: zero for a share that pays no dividend, the dividend yield for an index, the foreign interest rate for a currency, and \\(r\\) for a futures contract, in which case \\(a=1\\). The value at each node is the discounted risk-neutral expectation of the values at the two nodes that follow, or the value of immediate exercise if the option is American and that value is greater. In practice thirty or more steps are used, and the calculation is done by computer. Two refinements are in general use."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:16.2#7", "source_key": "fd-textbook:16.2", "chapter": 16, "section": "16.2", "heading": "Sensitivities from the tree", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 7, "title": "Chapter 16, Numerical Methods: Trees, Simulation, and Finite Differences — 16.2 Sensitivities from the tree", "body": "The tree that values an option also supplies its sensitivities. Let \\(f_{i,j}\\) be the value of the option at the node reached after \\(i\\) steps of which \\(j\\) were upward, where the price of the asset is \\(S_0u^{j}d^{\\,i-j}\\). Delta is the change in the value of the option between the two nodes at the first step, divided by the change in the price: \\[ \\Delta=\\frac{f_{1,1}-f_{1,0}}{S_0u-S_0d}. \\] Gamma is the change in delta. The three nodes at the second step provide two estimates of delta, one over the interval from \\(S_0\\) to \\(S_0u^{2}\\) and one over the interval from \\(S_0d^{2}\\) to \\(S_0\\), whose midpoints are separated by \\(h=\\tfrac12(S_0u^{2}-S_0d^{2})\\): \\[ \\Gamma=\\frac{1}{h}\\left[\\frac{f_{2,2}-f_{2,1}}{S_0u^{2}-S_0}-\\frac{f_{2,1}-f_{2,0}}{S_0-S_0d^{2}}\\right]. \\] Theta is obtained from the middle node at the second step, at which the price is again \\(S_0\\) and the time is later by \\(2\\Delta t\\): \\[ \\Theta=\\frac{f_{2,1}-f_{0,0}}{2\\Delta t}. \\] Vega and rho are found by valuing the option again with a slightly different volatility or interest rate and taking the difference."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:16.2#8", "source_key": "fd-textbook:16.2", "chapter": 16, "section": "16.2", "heading": "Sensitivities from the tree", "kind": "example", "ord": 8, "title": "Chapter 16, Numerical Methods: Trees, Simulation, and Finite Differences — 16.2 Sensitivities from the tree", "body": "Worked example 16.1 — The sensitivities of an American put\nA six-month American put on Northwind shares has a strike of $30. The share price is $28, the volatility 39 percent, and the risk-free rate 5 percent. A tree with five steps gives the values below. Estimate delta, gamma, and theta.\nNode \\((i,j)\\)(0,0)(1,0)(1,1)(2,0)(2,1)(2,2)\nShare price ($)28.000024.751231.675221.879428.000035.8327\nValue of the put ($)3.94825.78812.06978.12063.41470.6880\n1\nDelta. \\((2.0697-5.7881)/(31.6752-24.7512)=-3.7184/6.9240=-0.537\\).\n2\nGamma. Upper delta: \\((0.6880-3.4147)/(35.8327-28)=-0.3481\\). Lower delta: \\((3.4147-8.1206)/(28-21.8794)=-0.7689\\). \\(h=\\tfrac12(35.8327-21.8794)=6.9767\\). \\(\\Gamma=(-0.3481+0.7689)/6.9767=0.0603\\).\n3\nTheta. \\(\\Delta t=0.1\\), so that \\(\\Theta=(3.4147-3.9482)/0.2=-2.668\\) per year, or \\(-\\$0.0073\\) per calendar day.\n4\nAccuracy. A tree with 500 steps gives a value of $3.911, a delta of −0.529, a gamma of 0.0557, and a theta of −2.38. Five steps give the value within 1 percent and the sensitivities within about 10 percent. At the node (2,0) the value of the put, $8.1206, is its intrinsic value: early exercise is optimal there."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:16.2#9", "source_key": "fd-textbook:16.2", "chapter": 16, "section": "16.2", "heading": "The control variate technique", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 9, "title": "Chapter 16, Numerical Methods: Trees, Simulation, and Finite Differences — 16.2 The control variate technique", "body": "The error of a tree with a given number of steps is much the same for an American option as for the corresponding European option, because the two are valued on the same tree and differ only where early exercise occurs. The error for the European option is known exactly, since the Black–Scholes–Merton formula gives its true value. The control variate technique uses this knowledge. Let \\(f_A\\) and \\(f_E\\) be the values of the American and the European option from the tree, and \\(f_{\\text{BSM}}\\) the value of the European option from the formula. The improved estimate is \\[ f_A+(f_{\\text{BSM}}-f_E). \\] The error that the tree is known to make on the European option is assumed to be the error that it makes on the American option, and is removed."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:16.2#10", "source_key": "fd-textbook:16.2", "chapter": 16, "section": "16.2", "heading": "The control variate technique", "kind": "example", "ord": 10, "title": "Chapter 16, Numerical Methods: Trees, Simulation, and Finite Differences — 16.2 The control variate technique", "body": "Worked example 16.2 — Correcting a tree of twenty steps\nFor the put of Worked example 16.1, a tree with 20 steps values the American option at $3.9460 and the European option at $3.8450. The Black–Scholes–Merton value of the European put is $3.8091. Find the improved estimate.\n1\nError of the tree on the European put. \\(3.8450-3.8091=0.0359\\): the tree values it too high.\n2\nCorrection. \\(3.9460+(3.8091-3.8450)=3.9460-0.0359=\\$3.9101\\).\n3\nComparison. A tree with 5,000 steps gives $3.9104. The uncorrected value is in error by $0.0356 and the corrected value by $0.0003. Twenty steps with the correction are more accurate than several hundred without it."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:16.2#11", "source_key": "fd-textbook:16.2", "chapter": 16, "section": "16.2", "heading": "The control variate technique", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 11, "title": "Chapter 16, Numerical Methods: Trees, Simulation, and Finite Differences — 16.2 The control variate technique", "body": "⚙ Interactive figure 16.1 — The control variate technique\nThe value of the American put against the number of steps\nThe figure shows the value of the six-month American put on Northwind shares from trees of 5 to 100 steps, without and with the control variate correction, and a reference value from a corrected tree of 2,000 steps. Vary the strike and the volatility. The uncorrected values oscillate about the reference; the corrected values lie close to it from the start."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:16.2#12", "source_key": "fd-textbook:16.2", "chapter": 16, "section": "16.2", "heading": "The control variate technique", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 12, "title": "Chapter 16, Numerical Methods: Trees, Simulation, and Finite Differences — 16.2 The control variate technique", "body": "The general principle is of wide application. Where a problem that cannot be solved exactly resembles one that can, the numerical method is applied to both, and its measured error on the second is used to correct the first. Section 16.3 applies the same principle to simulation."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:16.3#13", "source_key": "fd-textbook:16.3", "chapter": 16, "section": "16.3", "heading": "Monte Carlo simulation", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 13, "title": "Chapter 16, Numerical Methods: Trees, Simulation, and Finite Differences — 16.3 Monte Carlo simulation", "body": "By Principle 10.1 the value of a derivative is the expected payoff in a risk-neutral world, discounted at the risk-free rate. An expected value can be estimated by sampling. Monte Carlo simulation values a derivative as follows.\nGenerate at random a path for the price of the underlying asset in a risk-neutral world.\nCompute the payoff of the derivative on that path.\nRepeat the first two steps many times.\nCompute the mean of the payoffs.\nDiscount the mean at the risk-free rate.\nFor a share that follows geometric Brownian motion, Result 11.2 supplies the price at a future date directly. In a risk-neutral world, \\[ S_T=S_0\\exp\\!\\left[\\left(r-q-\\tfrac12\\sigma^{2}\\right)T+\\sigma\\sqrt{T}\\,\\varepsilon\\right], \\] where \\(\\varepsilon\\) is a random draw from the standard normal distribution. When the payoff depends on the price at one date only, one draw is needed for each trial. When it depends on the path, the life of the derivative is divided into intervals of length \\(\\Delta t\\), and the same expression is applied to each interval in turn, with \\(\\Delta t\\) in place of \\(T\\), the price at the start of the interval in place of \\(S_0\\), and a new draw for each interval."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:16.3#14", "source_key": "fd-textbook:16.3", "chapter": 16, "section": "16.3", "heading": "Monte Carlo simulation", "kind": "example", "ord": 14, "title": "Chapter 16, Numerical Methods: Trees, Simulation, and Finite Differences — 16.3 Monte Carlo simulation", "body": "Worked example 16.3 — Five trials by hand\nValue the three-month European call on Northwind shares with a strike of $30 by simulation, using the five random draws 0.52, −1.13, 1.64, −0.27, and 0.88. The share price is $28, the volatility 39 percent, and the risk-free rate 5 percent.\n1\nThe sampling formula. \\((r-\\sigma^{2}/2)T=(0.05-0.07605)\\times0.25=-0.00651\\) and \\(\\sigma\\sqrt T=0.195\\), so that \\(S_T=28\\exp(-0.00651+0.195\\,\\varepsilon)\\).\n2\nPrices and payoffs.\nDraw \\(\\varepsilon\\)0.52−1.131.64−0.270.88\n\\(S_T\\) ($)30.78722.31738.30226.39233.026\nPayoff ($)0.78708.30203.026\n3\nMean and discounting. The mean payoff is \\((0.787+0+8.302+0+3.026)/5=2.423\\). The estimate of the value is \\(2.423e^{-0.0125}=\\$2.39\\).\n4\nAssessment. The true value is $1.52. Five trials are far too few: one large payoff, $8.30, dominates the mean. The usefulness of the method depends on a measure of how far such an estimate may be from the truth."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:16.3#15", "source_key": "fd-textbook:16.3", "chapter": 16, "section": "16.3", "heading": "The standard error", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 15, "title": "Chapter 16, Numerical Methods: Trees, Simulation, and Finite Differences — 16.3 The standard error", "body": "The estimate from a simulation is the mean of a random sample, and the theory of sampling applies to it. Let \\(M\\) be the number of trials, \\(\\bar{f}\\) the mean of the discounted payoffs, and \\(s\\) their standard deviation.\nResult 16.1 — The standard error of a simulation\nThe standard error of the estimate \\(\\bar f\\) is \\[ \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{M}}, \\] and a 95 percent confidence interval for the value of the derivative is \\[ \\bar f-\\frac{1.96\\,s}{\\sqrt M}\\;<\\;f\\;<\\;\\bar f+\\frac{1.96\\,s}{\\sqrt M}. \\] The error falls in proportion to the square root of the number of trials. To halve the error, the number of trials must be multiplied by four; to reduce it to one tenth, by one hundred.\nThis answers the opening question. To reduce a standard error from $0.04 to $0.01, a factor of 4, the number of trials must be multiplied by \\(4^{2}=16\\), from 10,000 to 160,000."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:16.3#16", "source_key": "fd-textbook:16.3", "chapter": 16, "section": "16.3", "heading": "The standard error", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 16, "title": "Chapter 16, Numerical Methods: Trees, Simulation, and Finite Differences — 16.3 The standard error", "body": "A simulation of 10,000 trials for the Northwind call illustrates the result. In one such simulation the mean of the discounted payoffs was $1.469 and their standard deviation $2.973. The standard error is \\(2.973/\\sqrt{10{,}000}=\\$0.030\\), and the 95 percent confidence interval runs from \\(1.469-1.96\\times0.030=\\$1.41\\) to $1.53. The true value, $1.52, lies within it. The standard deviation of the payoffs is about twice the value of the option. This is typical of options that are out of the money, which pay nothing on most paths and a large amount on a few, and it is the reason that simulation requires many trials."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:16.3#17", "source_key": "fd-textbook:16.3", "chapter": 16, "section": "16.3", "heading": "Reducing the variance", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 17, "title": "Chapter 16, Numerical Methods: Trees, Simulation, and Finite Differences — 16.3 Reducing the variance", "body": "Since the error falls only with the square root of the number of trials, methods that reduce the standard deviation \\(s\\) are valuable. They are called variance reduction procedures. Two are described here.\nThe antithetic variable technique uses each random draw twice. For each draw \\(\\varepsilon\\) the payoff is computed with \\(\\varepsilon\\) and again with \\(-\\varepsilon\\), and the two are averaged. When one of the two payoffs is above the true value, the other tends to be below it, and the average of the pair varies less than the average of two independent trials. In a simulation of 10,000 pairs for the Northwind call, the standard deviation of the pair averages was $1.833 and the standard error $0.018. Twenty thousand independent trials, which require the same number of payoffs to be computed, would give a standard error of \\(2.973/\\sqrt{20{,}000}=\\$0.021\\). The gain is real and modest for this option; it is greater for options that are in the money, whose payoffs are more nearly linear in \\(\\varepsilon\\)."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:16.3#18", "source_key": "fd-textbook:16.3", "chapter": 16, "section": "16.3", "heading": "Reducing the variance", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 18, "title": "Chapter 16, Numerical Methods: Trees, Simulation, and Finite Differences — 16.3 Reducing the variance", "body": "The control variate technique of Section 16.2 applies to simulation as it does to trees. Suppose that derivative A, which has no formula, resembles derivative B, which has one. Both are valued with the same random draws, giving the estimates \\(f_A^{*}\\) and \\(f_B^{*}\\), and the estimate \\(f_A^{*}+(f_B-f_B^{*})\\) is used, where \\(f_B\\) is the known value of B. The errors of the two simulations are highly correlated, because the same paths produced both, and most of the error is removed."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:16.3#19", "source_key": "fd-textbook:16.3", "chapter": 16, "section": "16.3", "heading": "Key terms", "kind": "term", "ord": 19, "title": "Chapter 16, Numerical Methods: Trees, Simulation, and Finite Differences — 16.3 Key terms", "body": "Key terms: Trial: one simulated path and the payoff computed from it. Standard error: the standard deviation of the estimate, \\(s/\\sqrt M\\). Path-dependent derivative: a derivative whose payoff depends on prices before expiration as well as at expiration. Asian option: an option whose payoff depends on the average price of the asset over a period; an average price call pays \\(\\max(S_{\\text{ave}}-K,0)\\)."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:16.3#20", "source_key": "fd-textbook:16.3", "chapter": 16, "section": "16.3", "heading": "Reducing the variance", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 20, "title": "Chapter 16, Numerical Methods: Trees, Simulation, and Finite Differences — 16.3 Reducing the variance", "body": "⚙ Interactive figure 16.2 — A Monte Carlo valuation\nThe estimate and its confidence interval as trials accumulate\nThe figure values a three-month call on Northwind shares with a strike of $30. Select the European call, whose true value is known, or an Asian call on the average of the thirteen weekly closing prices, which has no formula. Choose the number of trials and the sampling method, and draw new samples. The solid line is the estimate as the trials accumulate and the dotted lines are the limits of the 95 percent confidence interval."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:16.3#21", "source_key": "fd-textbook:16.3", "chapter": 16, "section": "16.3", "heading": "Reducing the variance", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 21, "title": "Chapter 16, Numerical Methods: Trees, Simulation, and Finite Differences — 16.3 Reducing the variance", "body": "The figure shows the two properties of the method. The estimate wanders about the true value and the interval narrows slowly: the improvement from 100 trials to 10,000 is striking, and that from 10,000 to 50,000 is slight. And the Asian call is valued with no more difficulty than the European call, although no formula for it exists. Its value, about $0.66, is less than half that of the European call, because an average of prices varies less than the final price.\nThe cost of a simulation rises in proportion to the number of underlying variables, whereas the cost of a tree or a grid rises as a power of that number. For derivatives that depend on many variables, simulation is the only practical method. Its principal weakness is early exercise. On a simulated path the value of continuing is not known, since the simulation has not looked beyond the present path. The least-squares method of Francis Longstaff and Eduardo Schwartz overcomes the difficulty by estimating the value of continuing from a regression across all the paths, and is the standard approach for American options on several variables.\nConcept check 16.1"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:16.3#22", "source_key": "fd-textbook:16.3", "chapter": 16, "section": "16.3", "heading": "Reducing the variance", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 22, "title": "Chapter 16, Numerical Methods: Trees, Simulation, and Finite Differences — 16.3 Reducing the variance", "body": "A simulation of 2,500 trials gives a mean discounted payoff of $6.40 with a standard deviation of $10.00. Which statement is correct?"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:16.4#23", "source_key": "fd-textbook:16.4", "chapter": 16, "section": "16.4", "heading": "Finite difference methods", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 23, "title": "Chapter 16, Numerical Methods: Trees, Simulation, and Finite Differences — 16.4 Finite difference methods", "body": "Result 12.1 states that the value \\(f\\) of every derivative on an asset satisfies the differential equation \\[ \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}+(r-q)S\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial S}+\\frac12\\sigma^{2}S^{2}\\frac{\\partial^{2}f}{\\partial S^{2}}=rf , \\] here written for an asset that provides income at the rate \\(q\\). A finite difference method solves the equation numerically. The life of the derivative is divided into \\(N\\) intervals of length \\(\\Delta t=T/N\\), and prices from zero to a level \\(S_{\\max}\\), high enough that the option is of negligible value or certain to be exercised there, are divided into \\(M\\) intervals of length \\(\\Delta S=S_{\\max}/M\\). The points \\((i\\Delta t,\\,j\\Delta S)\\) form a grid, and \\(f_{i,j}\\) denotes the value of the derivative at the point \\((i,j)\\)."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:16.4#24", "source_key": "fd-textbook:16.4", "chapter": 16, "section": "16.4", "heading": "Finite difference methods", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 24, "title": "Chapter 16, Numerical Methods: Trees, Simulation, and Finite Differences — 16.4 Finite difference methods", "body": "The values on three edges of the grid are known. For a put with strike \\(K\\), the values at expiration are \\(f_{N,j}=\\max(K-j\\Delta S,0)\\); the value when the price is zero is \\(K\\); and the value when the price is \\(S_{\\max}\\) is zero. The method fills in the interior of the grid, one date at a time, from expiration back to the present. The simplest version is the explicit finite difference method, in which each value is computed directly from three values at the following date.\nResult 16.2 — The explicit finite difference method\n\\[ f_{i,j}=a_j^{*}f_{i+1,j-1}+b_j^{*}f_{i+1,j}+c_j^{*}f_{i+1,j+1}, \\] where \\[ a_j^{*}=\\frac{\\tfrac12\\sigma^{2}j^{2}\\Delta t-\\tfrac12(r-q)j\\Delta t}{1+r\\Delta t},\\qquad b_j^{*}=\\frac{1-\\sigma^{2}j^{2}\\Delta t}{1+r\\Delta t},\\qquad c_j^{*}=\\frac{\\tfrac12\\sigma^{2}j^{2}\\Delta t+\\tfrac12(r-q)j\\Delta t}{1+r\\Delta t}. \\] For an American option, \\(f_{i,j}\\) is replaced by the value of immediate exercise where that is greater."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:16.4#25", "source_key": "fd-textbook:16.4", "chapter": 16, "section": "16.4", "heading": "Finite difference methods", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 25, "title": "Chapter 16, Numerical Methods: Trees, Simulation, and Finite Differences — 16.4 Finite difference methods", "body": "The result has a familiar form. The three coefficients sum to \\(1/(1+r\\Delta t)\\), which is a discount factor for one interval. The value at a node is therefore a discounted weighted average of the values at three nodes at the following date: the node below, the node at the same price, and the node above. The weights \\(a_j^{*}(1+r\\Delta t)\\), \\(b_j^{*}(1+r\\Delta t)\\), and \\(c_j^{*}(1+r\\Delta t)\\) may be read as the risk-neutral probabilities that the price falls by \\(\\Delta S\\), is unchanged, or rises by \\(\\Delta S\\) over the interval. The explicit method is a trinomial tree, in which each node leads to three nodes and not two, laid out on a rectangular grid."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:16.4#26", "source_key": "fd-textbook:16.4", "chapter": 16, "section": "16.4", "heading": "Finite difference methods", "kind": "example", "ord": 26, "title": "Chapter 16, Numerical Methods: Trees, Simulation, and Finite Differences — 16.4 Finite difference methods", "body": "Worked example 16.4 — One step of the explicit method\nThe six-month American put on Northwind shares, with a strike of $30, is valued on a grid with \\(\\Delta S=\\$2\\) and 100 time steps, so that \\(\\Delta t=0.005\\). Find the values one step before expiration at share prices of $28 and $30.\n1\nCoefficients at $28 (\\(j=14\\)). \\(\\tfrac12\\sigma^{2}j^{2}\\Delta t=\\tfrac12\\times0.1521\\times196\\times0.005=0.07453\\); \\(\\tfrac12rj\\Delta t=\\tfrac12\\times0.05\\times14\\times0.005=0.00175\\); \\(1+r\\Delta t=1.00025\\). \\(a^{*}=(0.07453-0.00175)/1.00025=0.0728\\); \\(b^{*}=(1-0.14906)/1.00025=0.8507\\); \\(c^{*}=(0.07453+0.00175)/1.00025=0.0763\\).\n2\nValue at $28. At expiration the put is worth $4, $2, and $0 at prices of $26, $28, and $30. \\(0.0728\\times4+0.8507\\times2+0.0763\\times0=1.9926\\). The value of immediate exercise is $2.0000, which is greater. The value at the node is $2.0000: early exercise is optimal.\n3\nValue at $30 (\\(j=15\\)). \\(a^{*}=(0.08556-0.00188)/1.00025=0.0837\\). The values at expiration at $28, $30, and $32 are $2, $0, and $0, so that the value is \\(0.0837\\times2=\\$0.1673\\). Immediate exercise is worth nothing, and the option is held.\n4"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:16.4#27", "source_key": "fd-textbook:16.4", "chapter": 16, "section": "16.4", "heading": "Finite difference methods", "kind": "example", "ord": 27, "title": "Chapter 16, Numerical Methods: Trees, Simulation, and Finite Differences — 16.4 Finite difference methods", "body": "Continuation. The same calculation at every price gives the values one step before expiration, from which the values two steps before are found, and so on for 100 steps. The value at $28 at the present date is $3.888. A finer grid, with \\(\\Delta S=\\$0.50\\) and 2,000 time steps, gives $3.909, against $3.910 from the tree of Worked example 16.2."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:16.4#28", "source_key": "fd-textbook:16.4", "chapter": 16, "section": "16.4", "heading": "Stability", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 28, "title": "Chapter 16, Numerical Methods: Trees, Simulation, and Finite Differences — 16.4 Stability", "body": "The interpretation of the weights as probabilities fails if one of them is negative. The weight on the middle node, \\(b_j^{*}\\), is negative when \\(\\sigma^{2}j^{2}\\Delta t>1\\). This occurs first at the highest prices on the grid, where \\(j\\) is largest. When weights are negative, small errors are magnified at each step and alternate in sign from one node to the next. After a sufficient number of steps the computed values bear no relation to the solution. The method is then said to be unstable. All the weights are non-negative, and the method is stable, if \\[ \\Delta t\\le\\frac{1}{\\sigma^{2}M^{2}}, \\] where \\(M=S_{\\max}/\\Delta S\\) is the number of price intervals, provided also that \\(\\sigma^{2}\\) exceeds \\(r-q\\), as is almost always the case."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:16.4#29", "source_key": "fd-textbook:16.4", "chapter": 16, "section": "16.4", "heading": "Stability", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 29, "title": "Chapter 16, Numerical Methods: Trees, Simulation, and Finite Differences — 16.4 Stability", "body": "The condition is demanding. For the grid of Worked example 16.4, with \\(S_{\\max}=\\$60\\) and \\(\\Delta S=\\$2\\), \\(M=30\\) and \\(\\Delta t\\le1/(0.1521\\times900)=0.0073\\) years, so that at least 69 time steps are needed for a life of six months. If \\(\\Delta S\\) is halved to improve the accuracy, \\(M\\) doubles and the largest permissible \\(\\Delta t\\) falls to one quarter: the number of time steps must be multiplied by four, and the total amount of computation by eight. Interactive figure 16.3 shows what happens when the condition is ignored."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:16.4#30", "source_key": "fd-textbook:16.4", "chapter": 16, "section": "16.4", "heading": "Stability", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 30, "title": "Chapter 16, Numerical Methods: Trees, Simulation, and Finite Differences — 16.4 Stability", "body": "⚙ Interactive figure 16.3 — Three-dimensional view\nThe solution on the grid, and its destruction by instability\nThe surface is the value of the American put computed by the explicit method at every point of the grid, for share prices from $10 to $50. Expiration is at the front, where the surface is the payoff, and the present date is at the back. Select the price interval, then reduce the number of time steps and release the control: when the stability condition is badly violated, spikes appear at high share prices and grow toward the back of the grid. Drag to rotate; scroll or pinch to zoom.\nTable equivalent of this figure"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:16.4#31", "source_key": "fd-textbook:16.4", "chapter": 16, "section": "16.4", "heading": "Implicit methods", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 31, "title": "Chapter 16, Numerical Methods: Trees, Simulation, and Finite Differences — 16.4 Implicit methods", "body": "The explicit method computes each value from three known values at the following date. The implicit finite difference method instead relates three unknown values at one date to one known value at the following date. The values at each date are then found together, by solving a set of simultaneous linear equations. The equations have a simple structure and are solved quickly. The advantage of the implicit method is that it is stable for every choice of \\(\\Delta t\\) and \\(\\Delta S\\), so that the time step may be chosen for accuracy alone. The Crank–Nicolson method is an average of the explicit and the implicit methods. It is also stable, and it converges more rapidly than either as \\(\\Delta t\\) is reduced. It is the method most often used in practice."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:16.4#32", "source_key": "fd-textbook:16.4", "chapter": 16, "section": "16.4", "heading": "Implicit methods", "kind": "warning", "ord": 32, "title": "Chapter 16, Numerical Methods: Trees, Simulation, and Finite Differences — 16.4 Implicit methods", "body": "Common error\nA numerical method always returns a number, and the number is not a value unless the method has converged. An unstable grid, a tree with too few steps, and a simulation with too few trials each produce a figure with as many decimal places as any other. The safeguards are those of this chapter: repeat the calculation with a finer step and confirm that the result changes little; state a standard error with every simulated value; and test the method on a derivative whose value is known."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:16.5#33", "source_key": "fd-textbook:16.5", "chapter": 16, "section": "16.5", "heading": "★ Derivation of the explicit method", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 33, "title": "Chapter 16, Numerical Methods: Trees, Simulation, and Finite Differences — 16.5 ★ Derivation of the explicit method", "body": "Optional section. This section obtains Result 16.2 from the differential equation by replacing each derivative with a difference between neighbouring points of the grid.\nAt the point \\((i,j)\\) the derivative with respect to time is approximated by the difference between the values at the following date and at the present date, \\[ \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}\\approx\\frac{f_{i+1,j}-f_{i,j}}{\\Delta t}. \\] The explicit method evaluates the derivatives with respect to the price at the following date, where all values are known: \\[ \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial S}\\approx\\frac{f_{i+1,j+1}-f_{i+1,j-1}}{2\\Delta S},\\qquad \\frac{\\partial^{2}f}{\\partial S^{2}}\\approx\\frac{f_{i+1,j+1}-2f_{i+1,j}+f_{i+1,j-1}}{\\Delta S^{2}}. \\] The second of these is the butterfly of Result 15.3. Substituting in the differential equation, with \\(S=j\\Delta S\\) and the right-hand side evaluated as \\(rf_{i,j}\\),\n\\[ \\frac{f_{i+1,j}-f_{i,j}}{\\Delta t}+(r-q)j\\,\\frac{f_{i+1,j+1}-f_{i+1,j-1}}{2}+\\frac12\\sigma^{2}j^{2}\\left(f_{i+1,j+1}-2f_{i+1,j}+f_{i+1,j-1}\\right)=rf_{i,j} . \\]"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:16.5#34", "source_key": "fd-textbook:16.5", "chapter": 16, "section": "16.5", "heading": "★ Derivation of the explicit method", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 34, "title": "Chapter 16, Numerical Methods: Trees, Simulation, and Finite Differences — 16.5 ★ Derivation of the explicit method", "body": "The interval \\(\\Delta S\\) has cancelled, since \\(S/\\Delta S=j\\) and \\(S^{2}/\\Delta S^{2}=j^{2}\\). Multiplying by \\(\\Delta t\\) and collecting the terms in \\(f_{i,j}\\) on the left gives \\[ (1+r\\Delta t)f_{i,j}=\\left[\\tfrac12\\sigma^{2}j^{2}-\\tfrac12(r-q)j\\right]\\Delta t\\,f_{i+1,j-1}+\\left[1-\\sigma^{2}j^{2}\\Delta t\\right]f_{i+1,j}+\\left[\\tfrac12\\sigma^{2}j^{2}+\\tfrac12(r-q)j\\right]\\Delta t\\,f_{i+1,j+1}, \\] which is Result 16.2. The implicit method differs only in evaluating the two derivatives with respect to the price at the present date \\(i\\), where the values are unknown; three unknowns then appear in each equation.\nThe three weights have a mean change in price of \\((r-q)S\\Delta t\\) and a variance of approximately \\(\\sigma^{2}S^{2}\\Delta t\\), which are the mean and variance of the change in the price in a risk-neutral world over the interval. The explicit method and the trinomial tree are the same calculation reached from two directions."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:16.6#35", "source_key": "fd-textbook:16.6", "chapter": 16, "section": "16.6", "heading": "The choice of method", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 35, "title": "Chapter 16, Numerical Methods: Trees, Simulation, and Finite Differences — 16.6 The choice of method", "body": "For a European option on one asset the formula is used, and no numerical method is required. For an American option on one asset a tree with the control variate correction, or a Crank–Nicolson grid, is used. For a derivative whose payoff depends on the path, or on several assets, Monte Carlo simulation is used, with variance reduction. For a derivative with both early exercise and several underlying variables, simulation with the least-squares method is used. Where two methods are available, each serves as a check on the other, and agreement between them is the best evidence that a value is correct.\nConcept check 16.2\nAn explicit finite difference grid for an option on a share with a volatility of 40 percent uses 50 price intervals. What is the largest time step for which every weight is non-negative, and what happens if the price interval is halved?\nConcept check 16.3\nA bank must value a European option that pays the amount by which the best-performing of eight share prices exceeds a strike. Which method is appropriate, and why?"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:16.6#36", "source_key": "fd-textbook:16.6", "chapter": 16, "section": "16.6", "heading": "The choice of method", "kind": "notes", "ord": 36, "title": "Chapter 16, Numerical Methods: Trees, Simulation, and Finite Differences — 16.6 The choice of method", "body": "Historical note\nThe Monte Carlo method was devised at Los Alamos in the 1940s by Stanislaw Ulam and John von Neumann for problems in the diffusion of neutrons that could not be solved analytically. Ulam is reported to have conceived the idea while estimating by repeated play the probability of winning a game of patience. Nicholas Metropolis proposed the name, after the casino. The finite difference methods of this chapter are older: the method of John Crank and Phyllis Nicolson was published in 1947 for the equation that governs the conduction of heat, to which the Black–Scholes–Merton equation can be reduced by a change of variables.\nAll three methods entered finance within a few years of the Black–Scholes–Merton paper. Phelim Boyle applied Monte Carlo simulation to options in 1977. Michael Brennan and Eduardo Schwartz applied finite differences to the American put in the same year. Cox, Ross, and Rubinstein published the binomial tree in 1979. The valuation of American options by simulation remained an open problem until the work of Longstaff and Schwartz in 2001."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:Summary#37", "source_key": "fd-textbook:Summary", "chapter": 16, "section": "Summary", "heading": "Summary", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 37, "title": "Chapter 16, Numerical Methods: Trees, Simulation, and Finite Differences — Summary Summary", "body": "Trees and finite difference methods work backward from expiration and handle early exercise. Monte Carlo simulation works forward and handles payoffs that depend on the path and derivatives on many variables.\nA tree supplies delta, gamma, and theta from its first two steps. The control variate technique corrects the tree value of an American option by the known error of the same tree on the European option.\nMonte Carlo simulation averages the discounted payoffs of randomly generated paths. Its standard error is \\(s/\\sqrt M\\), so that four times as many trials are needed to halve the error.\nAntithetic variables and control variates reduce the variance of a simulation.\nThe explicit finite difference method computes each value as a discounted weighted average of three values at the following date, and is a trinomial tree. It is stable only if \\(\\Delta t\\le1/(\\sigma^{2}M^{2})\\). The implicit and Crank–Nicolson methods are stable for every time step.\nA numerical result should be accepted only when refinement of the step, a stated standard error, or agreement with a second method shows that it has converged."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:Exercises#38", "source_key": "fd-textbook:Exercises", "chapter": 16, "section": "Exercises", "heading": "Exercises", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 38, "title": "Chapter 16, Numerical Methods: Trees, Simulation, and Finite Differences — Exercises Exercises", "body": "16.1 A stock index has a volatility of 20 percent and a dividend yield of 2 percent. The risk-free rate is 5 percent. Find \\(u\\), \\(d\\), and \\(p\\) for a tree with a time step of three months.\n\\(u=e^{0.20\\sqrt{0.25}}=e^{0.10}=1.1052\\); \\(d=1/u=0.9048\\); \\(a=e^{(0.05-0.02)\\times0.25}=1.0075\\); \\(p=(1.0075-0.9048)/(1.1052-0.9048)=0.1027/0.2003=0.5126\\).\n16.2 A tree for an American call on a share priced at $60 has \\(u=1.10\\) and \\(d=0.9091\\), with a time step of one month. The values of the option are $5.20 at the initial node, $8.90 and $2.40 at the upper and lower nodes after one step, and $13.60, $4.90, and $0.70 at the three nodes after two steps. Estimate delta, gamma, and theta.\nPrices after one step: $66.00 and $54.55. Delta: \\((8.90-2.40)/(66.00-54.55)=6.50/11.45=0.568\\). Prices after two steps: $72.60, $60.00, and $49.59. Upper delta: \\((13.60-4.90)/12.60=0.6905\\). Lower delta: \\((4.90-0.70)/10.41=0.4035\\). \\(h=\\tfrac12(72.60-49.59)=11.505\\). Gamma: \\((0.6905-0.4035)/11.505=0.0249\\). Theta: \\((4.90-5.20)/(2/12)=-1.80\\) per year, or about \\(-\\$0.0049\\) per calendar day."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:Exercises#39", "source_key": "fd-textbook:Exercises", "chapter": 16, "section": "Exercises", "heading": "Exercises", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 39, "title": "Chapter 16, Numerical Methods: Trees, Simulation, and Finite Differences — Exercises Exercises", "body": "16.3 A tree with 30 steps values an American put at $4.26 and the corresponding European put at $3.94. The Black–Scholes–Merton value of the European put is $3.99. Find the control variate estimate of the value of the American put.\nThe tree undervalues the European put by \\(3.99-3.94=\\$0.05\\). The estimate is \\(4.26+0.05=\\$4.31\\).\n16.4 A simulation of 40,000 trials gives a mean discounted payoff of $5.84 and a standard deviation of $9.20. (a) Find the standard error and the 95 percent confidence interval. (b) How many trials would be needed for a standard error of $0.01?\n(a) \\(9.20/\\sqrt{40{,}000}=9.20/200=\\$0.046\\). The interval is \\(5.84\\pm1.96\\times0.046\\), from $5.75 to $5.93. (b) \\(M=(s/0.01)^{2}=920^{2}=846{,}400\\) trials.\n16.5 A share that pays no dividend trades at $50 and has a volatility of 30 percent. The risk-free rate is 4 percent. A random draw of 0.80 is obtained. Using the antithetic variable technique, find the two simulated prices in six months and the pair's estimate of the value of a six-month European call with a strike of $50."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:Exercises#40", "source_key": "fd-textbook:Exercises", "chapter": 16, "section": "Exercises", "heading": "Exercises", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 40, "title": "Chapter 16, Numerical Methods: Trees, Simulation, and Finite Differences — Exercises Exercises", "body": "\\((r-\\sigma^{2}/2)T=(0.04-0.045)\\times0.5=-0.0025\\); \\(\\sigma\\sqrt T=0.2121\\). With \\(\\varepsilon=0.80\\): \\(S_T=50e^{-0.0025+0.1697}=50e^{0.1672}=\\$59.10\\); payoff $9.10. With \\(\\varepsilon=-0.80\\): \\(S_T=50e^{-0.1722}=\\$42.09\\); payoff 0. The average payoff is $4.55, and the estimate is \\(4.55e^{-0.02}=\\$4.46\\). The Black–Scholes–Merton value, from Exercise 12.1, is $4.70.\n16.6 A one-year option on a share with a volatility of 30 percent is to be valued by the explicit finite difference method, on a grid that extends to a share price of $200 in intervals of $5. (a) Find the smallest number of time steps for which the method is stable. (b) Find the number if the interval is reduced to $2.50.\n(a) \\(M=200/5=40\\). \\(\\Delta t\\le1/(0.09\\times1{,}600)=0.006944\\) years, so that \\(N\\ge1/0.006944=144\\) steps. (b) \\(M=80\\). \\(\\Delta t\\le1/(0.09\\times6{,}400)=0.001736\\), so that \\(N\\ge576\\) steps, four times as many."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:Exercises#41", "source_key": "fd-textbook:Exercises", "chapter": 16, "section": "Exercises", "heading": "Exercises", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 41, "title": "Chapter 16, Numerical Methods: Trees, Simulation, and Finite Differences — Exercises Exercises", "body": "16.7 In an explicit finite difference grid for an American put with a strike of $50, the coefficients at the node for a share price of $46 are \\(a^{*}=0.12\\), \\(b^{*}=0.76\\), and \\(c^{*}=0.115\\). The values at the following date at share prices of $44, $46, and $48 are $6.30, $4.60, and $3.20. Find the value at the node.\nThe value of continuing is \\(0.12\\times6.30+0.76\\times4.60+0.115\\times3.20=0.756+3.496+0.368=\\$4.62\\). The value of immediate exercise is \\(50-46=\\$4.00\\). The value at the node is the greater, $4.62, and the option is held.\n16.8 State, with a reason, the numerical method best suited to each of the following: (a) a one-year American put on a share that pays no dividend; (b) a one-year European option on the average of the daily closing prices of a share; (c) a European option on the lowest of the returns on five stock indices."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:Exercises#42", "source_key": "fd-textbook:Exercises", "chapter": 16, "section": "Exercises", "heading": "Exercises", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 42, "title": "Chapter 16, Numerical Methods: Trees, Simulation, and Finite Differences — Exercises Exercises", "body": "(a) A binomial tree with the control variate correction, or a Crank–Nicolson grid: there is one underlying variable and early exercise, which a backward method handles directly. (b) Monte Carlo simulation: the payoff depends on the path, which a forward method follows directly; the corresponding option on the geometric average, which has a formula, may serve as a control variate. (c) Monte Carlo simulation: there are five underlying variables, and the cost of a tree or a grid in five dimensions is prohibitive."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:Further reading#43", "source_key": "fd-textbook:Further reading", "chapter": 16, "section": "Further reading", "heading": "Further reading", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 43, "title": "Chapter 16, Numerical Methods: Trees, Simulation, and Finite Differences — Further reading Further reading", "body": "Boyle, P. P. (1977). Options: A Monte Carlo approach. Journal of Financial Economics, 4(3), 323–338.\nBrennan, M. J., and Schwartz, E. S. (1977). The valuation of American put options. Journal of Finance, 32(2), 449–462.\nGlasserman, P. (2004). Monte Carlo Methods in Financial Engineering. Springer.\nHull, J. C. (2022). Options, Futures, and Other Derivatives (11th ed.). Pearson. Chapter 21.\nLongstaff, F. A., and Schwartz, E. S. (2001). Valuing American options by simulation: A simple least-squares approach. Review of Financial Studies, 14(1), 113–147.\nWilmott, P., Howison, S., and Dewynne, J. (1995). The Mathematics of Financial Derivatives. Cambridge University Press."}]$aim_json$::jsonb, 'Financial Derivatives (MacroNations textbook)');
